# SYNOPSIS

```swift
import Kit          // UICore, which it re-exports
import KitPlatform  // on the device: a Kit app in a window

@MainActor protocol App: AnyObject {
  var title: String { get }
  var actions: [ActionHandler] { get }
  @ViewBuilder var body: Group { get }
  func prepare(width: Float, height: Float)     // optional
  var busy: Bool { get }                        // optional
  func route(subject: String) -> Route?         // optional
  func takeInput(_ input: FrameInput) -> Bool   // optional
}

struct ActionHandler {
  init(_ action: Action, run: @escaping @MainActor (Invocation) async -> Void)
}
struct Binding<Value> {
  init<Model: AnyObject>(_ model: Model,
                         _ path: ReferenceWritableKeyPath<Model, Value>)
  init(get: @escaping () -> Value, set: @escaping (Value) -> Void)
}
// Views: Text, VStack, HStack, Window, Spacer, Button, TextField, List,
// Drawing, ScrollBar, TextEditor, MenuBar, Icon, ForEach; .id(_:)

@MainActor final class Runner {
  init(app: any App, painter: Painter, width: Float, height: Float)
  func frame(_ input: FrameInput) -> Bool
  var wantsFrame: Bool { get }
  var running: [String] { get }
}

@MainActor func run(_ app: any App, fonts: Fonts? = nil,
                    options: RunOptions = RunOptions()) async throws

@MainActor final class Session {
  init(fonts: Fonts? = nil, theme: Theme? = nil) throws
  func open(_ app: any App, width: Int32, height: Int32,
            at place: (x: Int32, y: Int32)? = nil,
            desktop: Bool = false, panel: Bool = false) throws -> KitWindow
  func run() async
  var theme: Theme { get }
  var fonts: Fonts { get }
  func retheme(_ t: Theme)
  func route(_ r: Route) throws(RouteError)
  func listen(port: String) throws(RouteError) -> ListenID
  var afterFrame: ((KitWindow) -> Bool)?
  var onKey: ((KitWindow, VX.Key, _ down: Bool) -> Bool)?
  func listID(_ w: KitWindow) -> UInt32?
  func openView(_ app: any App, width: Int32, height: Int32) throws -> KitWindow
  func adoptView(_ app: any App, opened: consuming RadharcIPC.Opened) throws
    -> KitWindow
  func viewToken(_ w: KitWindow) -> Handle?
  func embed(_ host: KitWindow, token: consuming Handle, id: UInt32,
             x: Int32, y: Int32, width: Int32, height: Int32)
    throws(WindowError)
  func placeEmbedding(_ host: KitWindow, id: UInt32, x: Int32, y: Int32,
                      width: Int32, height: Int32) throws(WindowError)
  func unembed(_ host: KitWindow, id: UInt32)
  var onViewGone: ((KitWindow, UInt32) -> Void)?
  func showClock(_ w: KitWindow, x: Int32, y: Int32, width: Int32,
                 height: Int32, hours24: Bool)
  var waker: @Sendable () -> Void { get }
  var wakeups: UInt64 { get }
  static func installedFonts() -> [Font]
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

Kit is a way of writing UICore's immediate mode (uicore(2)), not a second toolkit.
An app is a class on the main actor: its `body` is evaluated again each frame, and its views build UICore's boxes, each keyed by its place among its siblings or by `.id`, so what lives underneath (scroll offsets, carets, focus) keeps across frames.
A view reads the app's model as it is built, and a `Binding` writes back into it: a text field's text, a list's selection.
An app declares its actions once, each with its handler, and controls, keys and menus invoke them; a handler runs on the main actor, as a task if it is async, and while one runs, more invocations of the same action wait for it.
When a handler ends, the runner asks for a frame.
A window whose content does not fit scrolls with bars; a panel or the desktop does not.
A context menu's Route sends the app's `route(subject:)`, by default the subject as text, to the plumber.

Kit is full Swift, tier 1 (D47), and so only on amd64 and arm64.
`KitPlatform` runs Kit apps in windows of Radharc's over the SDK (sdk(2)).
`run` opens one app's window and returns when it closes; a `Session` holds several windows on one loop, each at a place of its own (`at`) or as the desktop or a panel.
The SDK's events become the frame's input: the pointer and its buttons in points, the wheel, keys, and the text a key types, its character shifted by kbdfs's keymap (kbdfs(4)) under Shift, and a letter's under Caps Lock, which Shift undoes.
A frame is built when something happened, and drawn on the CPU into the window's canvas; only what changed is drawn and copied into the buffer presented, which is presented only if the frame changed.
`openView` runs an app in a view, which another program's window embeds by its token (`viewToken`), as rio(1) shows vt(1)'s, and `adoptView` runs one in a view Radharc opened and handed over, as tracker(4) runs its chooser inside the trusted prompt; `embed`, `placeEmbedding` and `unembed` are the SDK's (sdk(2)), and `onViewGone` says when a view a window shows has closed.
`showClock` is the SDK's too: Radharc draws the time in the window, as deskbar(4)'s.
Each window's fields and text areas cut, copy and paste through the clipboard, the SDK's `Snarf` (sdk(2)).
`onKey` hears each key down and up as the SDK gives it, before the app does, and takes it by returning true (rio(1)'s `kbd`); a window's `configuration` is its last configure, and `listID` its id in Radharc's window list.
An idle window's loop waits with no deadline, so it wakes for nothing; while an action's async handler runs, it waits at most 4 ms at a time and yields to it.

A session with no theme named draws in the one Radharc has in force and follows it: on each change every window is drawn again in the new theme and its faces.
With no fonts given, its faces are those the theme's typefaces name (theme(6)), chosen from `/lib/fonts` (`installedFonts`: a directory a family, a file a face), which a manifest mounts with `mount /lib/fonts bootfs/lib/fonts`; the desktop's images carry data/fonts there with their licences.

`KitSample` is board 02's live query, written with Kit, and `render` draws one frame of it, after a few frames of typing, in a window's chrome on the desk, in a theme and its faces.
KitTests on the host draw it in each shipped theme and compare the drawing's digest (FNV-1a over its pixels) with `ui/kit/tests/kit-sample.tsv`; a drawing that differs is written to `.build/kit-sample/`, and `VX_KIT_RECORD=1` records them anew.
The boot test boot-kit runs bin/kit-test in the desktop, which draws the sample on the device and compares it with the same digests, then opens a form in a window: it checks its background on the screen, types into its field through QEMU's keys, submits it, copies its text and pastes it after with Control-A, C and V, reads the clipboard back, runs an async action from Control-S and presses a button with a click; it loads Neon Tab through `theme/ctl`, sees the window redrawn and the sample drawn as recorded in Neon Tab's faces, loads Platinum Indigo again, and checks that its idle loop wakes only for a wake.

